{
  "identifier": "NG4444",
  "full_title": "The Virgin and Child with Four Saints and Twelve Devotees",
  "short_title": "The Virgin and Child with Four Saints and Twelve Devotees",
  "object_name": "Picture",
  "style": "early Renaissance",
  "material": "egg tempera on wood",
  "dimensions": "55.7 × 48.8 cm",
  "production_date": "probably about 1490-5",
  "brief_description": "In this miniaturised altarpiece, the Virgin Mary sits with an enormous Christ Child on a gilded throne. They are flanked by four saints, and in the foreground six men and six women kneel in prayer, the men on the Virgin's right-hand -- and more honourable -- side. They are stylishly dressed in the fashions of the 1490s. They could perhaps be members of a single family, arranged behind a married couple who face each other, or possibly members of a confraternity (quasi-religious brotherhood).\n\nWe don't know who the artist was, although he was a follower of Leonardo da Vinci: Mary's head here seems to be based on that in Leonardo's 'The Virgin of the Rocks'. The extensive use of gold leaf is typical of the Lombard region. Much of the decorative effect is now lost, but various techniques were used to produce subtle variations in texture and surface.",
  "accession_date": "1929-01-01",
  "current_location": "Not on display",
  "credit_line": "Presented by Lady Margaret Watney in memory of her husband, Vernon J. Watney, 1929",
